{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792489323,"nonce":"Ave8LrlKaFUVXlB9r4V+D+8MI3k=","title":"Inline flows keep the owners of their line pieces and box copies after the flow drops them","message":"An inline flow makes a Text for each line of each run (a Piece) and a Div for each extra line of a decorated inline element (a DecoBox copy). Both were made with `Owner.root(tree, _ -\u003e ...)`, which throws away the function that disposes that owner. So when a run left the flow, or the flow's own owner was disposed, the node went but the owner did not: Text.byNode kept the Text, and the signals and bindings the piece holds stayed alive.\n\n**Reproduction**\n\nA `\u003cfor {name in [file.get()]}\u003e` whose body is a `\u003cbreadcrumb\u003e` of `\u003cbreadcrumb-item\u003e`s, with `file` set twenty times. Text.byNode grew by one Text for each item each time (88 entries against 8 for one breadcrumb). A plain `\u003ctext\u003e` in the same `\u003cfor\u003e` did not leak, so the cause was the flow's pieces, not Text. In RayDE, opening 30 files and closing all of them left 135 breadcrumb Texts in the registry above the count of a window with no tabs.\n\n**Fix**\n\nPiece and DecoBox keep the function that stops their owner and call it when the piece or copy is removed, and InlineFlow.forget() calls it for those still held when the flow goes. tests/fixtures/components/InlineFlowRelease.hx fails on the old code and passes now.\n\n**What it does not explain**\n\nRayDE with every tab closed still held about 100 MB over a fresh window (424 against 327 MB footprint) after the leak was fixed, and about 265,000 more live malloc blocks. Allocation call trees put most of that growth in the Ash runtime's JIT (cranelift and LLVM structures, LLVM debug-info metadata), Haxe hash tables and the allocator's high-water, with about 6 MB in blinc_abi. The registries of ashui all return to the count of an empty window once this leak is fixed, except for about 10 Text entries still unexplained.","files":null}]}